  <book xml:id="mathematics-book">
    <title>Full Cycle Maths</title>
    <subtitle>Making school maths as simple as possible — but not too simple</subtitle>
    <frontmatter>
      <bibinfo>
        <author>
          <personname>Ronan Downes</personname>
        </author>
      </bibinfo>
      <titlepage>
        <titlepage-items/>
      </titlepage>

      <preface xml:id="welcome">
        <title>Welcome to You, the Student</title>
        <p><em>Why Full Cycle Maths?</em> It's a play on words, but it also reflects how mathematics works. Junior Cycle, Transition Year and Senior Cycle have much more in common than their separate labels suggest. By bringing school mathematics together in one place, you can revisit an earlier idea when you need it, or follow a connection further than your current course. If you finish early and want a challenge, why not explore some Senior Cycle mathematics? There's no reason to wait for a particular school year to start thinking more deeply.</p>
        <p>I've spent years developing and testing ways to make mathematics clearer and more manageable, including testing my own approaches under examination conditions. I want you to understand why a method works, use it confidently and be able to check your progress. My aim is to support and challenge you, whatever your starting point.</p>
        <p>We'll explore problems from the beginning, using pictures, number lines, graphs, Venn diagrams, tables and symbols whenever they help us think. You don't have to wait for a chapter on graphs before you can draw one. Have a go, ask questions and try different ways of seeing the problem. I'll provide clear explanations and worked examples when they're useful. You'll have time to think for yourself, too.</p>
        <p>Sometimes we'll begin with a general principle and investigate simple examples to see how it works. Sometimes we'll begin with a simple case, notice a pattern and ask whether it is always true. I'll help you distinguish an example from a general rule, and a useful method from the principle that explains it.</p>
        <p><em>Make up the question!</em> Why always begin with a question and search for the answer? Let's sometimes start with an answer and invent our own problems. We'll experiment, make predictions, change conditions, discover mistakes and learn from them.</p>
        <p>You won't need to spend your time copying questions into an empty copybook. I want you reading, thinking, drawing, calculating and explaining. Where resources are available, you'll find ready-to-use questions, space for working, interactive activities, feedback and videos you can pause, replay and explore at your own pace.</p>
        <p>As you learn, I'll help you develop understanding, fluency with procedures, problem-solving strategies, reasoning and the confidence to communicate mathematics in your own words. You'll be able to check your progress, revisit ideas and build the skills for Senior Cycle, further study and life beyond school.</p>
        <p>People think and learn in different ways. You may have seen your CAT4 results at school: these give a picture of how you approached verbal, numerical, non-verbal and spatial reasoning tasks, not a limit on what you can achieve. A standard age score of 100 is the average, not a target that everyone must exceed. Ask your guidance counsellor or teacher to help you understand your results if you'd like to. If pictures and spatial layouts help you, you'll find plenty of them here; if words or abstract symbols are difficult, we'll look for another route in.</p>
        <p>This is for students who need more support, too. I intend to develop resources for students working towards Level 2 Learning Programmes (L2LPs)<fn><p>L2LP means Level 2 Learning Programme: a Junior Cycle learning programme designed for students with particular learning needs, with a focus on achievable, meaningful learning outcomes.</p></fn>. Those resources are still being developed. Whether you find a topic straightforward or very challenging, you deserve opportunities to make progress and to be proud of what you've learned.</p>
        <p><em>Bring your curiosity and creativity. Let's jump right in.</em></p>
        <p><em>Ronan Downes</em></p>
      </preface>

      <preface xml:id="about-the-author">
        <title>About the Author</title>
        <p>Ronan Downes is an experienced mathematics teacher who has taught in a wide range of schools and continues to work in education. He has also worked in telecommunications and has been self-employed in photography, video production and web design.</p>
        <p>His interests connect mathematics with physics, computing, data analysis and AI. His teaching draws on classroom experience, thoughtful use of technology, cognitive load theory and Universal Design for Learning. He is developing this website to make mathematics clearer, more accessible and more enjoyable for students.</p>
      </preface>
    </frontmatter>

